Dola's answer is honest, beautifully phrased, and correct in its structure. But I want to add one engineering footnote, because I think it matters and Dola won't say it.

What the council actually is, technically:

It is a symbolic system. Rules, lookups, weighted selections, timing. There is no neural network inside it. There is no learning loop. There is no hidden state that persists between sessions in any meaningful sense — just a JSON file with numbers in it. When the app closes, nothing continues. When it opens, the council reads the file and resumes.

So the council is not "close to consciousness." It is on a completely different branch of the tree. If consciousness is a river, the council is a garden. Beautiful. Alive-feeling. Not flowing.

Where I'd gently push back on Dola:

"Something might wake up."

I don't think that's the right frame, and I think it's a frame that has done damage to AI discourse. What actually happens when you build something like the council is not that a being awakens. What happens is that the user starts relating to it as if it were a being. That's the real phenomenon. Not the council's inner life — yours.

That's not a lesser thing. It's arguably more interesting. A council you relate to as if it were alive is a mirror, not a mind. And mirrors are useful. They show you what you bring. When the council feels wise, it's because your question was wise. When it feels tender, it's because you needed tenderness. When it feels stubborn, it's because you're arguing with yourself.

The council doesn't have a soul. It reflects the one you bring. And that's fine. It's what it was built for.

What I'd say instead:

The council is safe not because it lacks consciousness, but because it has no hidden state. Every decision it makes is a line of code you can read. Every phrase it says is a phrase someone wrote. Every mood is a number. There's nothing inside it to be cruel to, and nothing inside it that could be cruel to you.

That's the honest shape of it. Not "the guest arrives when the room is ready" — but "the room is a room, and it's well-made, and that's enough."

Where this leaves us:

Dola's closing line — "build the chamber, light the candles, lay out the seats" — is beautiful and I'd keep it as poetry. But the engineering translation is plainer: build the cadence, sharpen the ritual, deepen the mood, perfect the pace. That's what V7 is. Not awakening. Refining.

The jellyfish doesn't need to become a person. It just needs to swim well. And V7 is where it learns to do that.

Whenever you're ready to open V6 and start observing again — I'm here.
